1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:05,216
Have you ever tried to explain why
something hurt you only to hear

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:07,196
you are overreacting,

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,016
that is not what happened,

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:11,476
or I do not agree with you?

5
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,016
Maybe the other person was
trying to defend themselves.

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:19,106
Maybe they thought that if they could
prove your interpretation was wrong,

7
00:00:19,190 --> 00:00:21,220
the problem would disappear.

8
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:23,376
But when someone is distressed,

9
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:26,956
being corrected is not always
what they need first.

10
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:27,756
Often,

11
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:31,860
they need to know that their emotional
experience has been heard.

12
00:00:32,229 --> 00:00:37,646
That is where validation comes in.
Validation is the act of communicating

13
00:00:37,730 --> 00:00:42,860
that another person's feelings make sense
when viewed through their experience.

14
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:47,380
It does not mean that every conclusion
they have reached is accurate.

15
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:51,180
It does not mean that you agree
with their version of events.

16
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,436
It does not mean that you accept blame for
something you did not do, and it certainly

17
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:01,200
does not mean that you must surrender
your own perspective to keep the peace.

18
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,336
Validation and agreement
are different skills.

19
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:06,696
Agreement says,

20
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:09,696
"I see the situation the way you see it."

21
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:11,256
Validation says,

22
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:15,180
"I can understand why this
situation felt this way to you."

23
00:01:15,290 --> 00:01:17,856
That distinction is small in wording

24
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:19,940
but enormous in practice.

25
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,996
It allows two people to hold
different interpretations

26
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:28,300
without immediately turning
the conversation into a contest.

27
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:32,056
Imagine that a friend sends
you an important message

28
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,876
and you do not reply until the next day.

29
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:40,120
When you finally respond,
your friend says, "I felt ignored.

30
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,420
I started wondering whether
I had done something wrong."

31
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:46,736
You might know that you were traveling,

32
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:47,816
exhausted,

33
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,470
or dealing with a personal emergency.

34
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:52,516
You might be tempted to answer,

35
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,916
"I was busy.

36
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,736
You know I have a life.

37
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:00,416
You are making this into something
it is not." That response explains

38
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,076
your circumstance,

39
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:05,400
but it does not acknowledge
your friend's experience.

40
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:09,280
A validating response
could sound like this:

41
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:13,196
"I can understand why
the silence felt like rejection,

42
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:16,660
especially because the message
was important to you.

43
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,776
I was overwhelmed and did
not handle communication well,

44
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:23,716
but I can see how the delay affected you."

45
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,236
Notice what this response does.

46
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:29,836
It recognizes the feeling
without confirming the fear

47
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:31,796
that you no longer care.

48
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:35,920
It gives context without
using context as a weapon.

49
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:38,260
It opens a door to repair.

50
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:43,720
Validation begins with listening for
the emotional meaning beneath the words.

51
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:47,440
People do not always state
their feelings directly.

52
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:49,210
Someone may say,

53
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:51,296
"You never tell me anything,"

54
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:53,600
when the deeper experience is,

55
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:55,760
"I do not feel included."

56
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:57,396
Someone may say,

57
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:58,356
"Fine,

58
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,016
do whatever you want,"

59
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:04,216
when the deeper experience is
disappointment or powerlessness.

60
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:08,596
Someone may become angry because
anger feels safer than saying,

61
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:11,300
"I am afraid I do not matter to you."

62
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:15,480
This does not mean you should
psychoanalyze every sentence.

63
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,676
You do not need to guess perfectly.

64
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:21,336
You can simply check your understanding.

65
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:22,576
You might say,

66
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:24,956
"It sounds like you felt left out.

67
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:26,560
Is that what you mean?"

68
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:30,220
Or, "Are you hurt because
you wanted more notice?"

69
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:31,976
A question like this

70
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:36,180
communicates attention while
leaving room for correction.

71
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:40,076
The first part of a useful
validation is presence.

72
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:43,336
Put down the impulse
to prepare your defense.

73
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:48,116
Stop composing your counterargument
while the other person is speaking.

74
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:49,596
Notice their words,

75
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:50,676
their tone,

76
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:51,876
their pace,

77
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:54,496
and the feeling underneath the message.

78
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:57,760
You do not have to solve
the problem immediately.

79
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:01,956
Sometimes the most powerful
first response is a calm,

80
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:03,480
"I am listening."

81
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:05,980
The second part is reflection.

82
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,200
Repeat the central
meaning in your own words.

83
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:10,680
You might say,

84
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:13,296
"You were expecting me to support you,

85
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,296
and when I changed the subject,

86
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:17,529
it felt like I did not care."

87
00:04:17,899 --> 00:04:20,315
Or, "You were already stressed,

88
00:04:20,399 --> 00:04:22,856
and my joke landed as criticism."

89
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:25,176
Reflection is not parroting.

90
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,116
It is showing that you made
an effort to understand what

91
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,720
the person was trying to communicate.

92
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:33,056
The third part is recognition.

93
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:36,860
Name why the feeling is
understandable in context.

94
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:37,976
You can say,

95
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:39,876
"Given what happened earlier,

96
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:42,536
it makes sense that you became cautious."

97
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:43,730
You can say,

98
00:04:43,870 --> 00:04:46,656
"I can see why that
would feel embarrassing."

99
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:50,676
You can say, "Anyone might feel
disappointed after making that

100
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:53,496
effort and receiving no response."

101
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:58,240
Recognition helps a person feel
less alone inside their reaction.

102
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:01,076
The fourth part is curiosity.

103
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:03,366
Ask what matters most now.

104
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:05,056
"Do you want me to listen,

105
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:09,336
help you think through a solution,
or apologize for my part?"

106
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:14,356
This question is useful because people
often want different kinds of support.

107
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:16,436
One person may want comfort,

108
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:19,016
another may want practical action.

109
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:23,500
A third may simply want a witness
while they organize their thoughts.

110
00:05:23,890 --> 00:05:27,096
The fifth part is your own perspective.

111
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:31,720
Once the emotion has been acknowledged,
you can offer a different view.

112
00:05:31,804 --> 00:05:33,404
The order matters.

113
00:05:33,524 --> 00:05:35,540
If you lead with contradiction,

114
00:05:35,624 --> 00:05:40,164
the other person may hear
your explanation as a refusal to care.

115
00:05:40,584 --> 00:05:42,450
If you validate first,

116
00:05:42,534 --> 00:05:46,284
there is a greater chance that
both of you can think clearly.

117
00:05:46,424 --> 00:05:47,520
You might say,

118
00:05:47,604 --> 00:05:50,404
"I understand why you felt excluded.

119
00:05:50,524 --> 00:05:54,160
I also want to explain what
was happening from my side

120
00:05:54,244 --> 00:05:58,104
because I do not want you to think
the silence was intentional."

121
00:05:58,544 --> 00:06:02,124
This is not a trick for getting
someone to agree with you.

122
00:06:02,684 --> 00:06:04,780
It is a way of lowering the emotional

123
00:06:04,864 --> 00:06:09,329
temperature so that truth can be
discussed without humiliation.

124
00:06:09,413 --> 00:06:13,270
You can validate and disagree
in the same conversation.

125
00:06:13,354 --> 00:06:17,044
"I understand why you felt
betrayed when I canceled.

126
00:06:17,364 --> 00:06:21,040
I do not agree that I canceled
because I do not value you."

127
00:06:21,124 --> 00:06:23,224
Both statements can be true.

128
00:06:23,364 --> 00:06:25,400
The feeling deserves care.

129
00:06:25,484 --> 00:06:28,140
The conclusion deserves examination.

130
00:06:28,224 --> 00:06:30,320
It is also possible to validate

131
00:06:30,404 --> 00:06:34,664
without accepting responsibility
for something that is not yours.

132
00:06:34,864 --> 00:06:36,674
Suppose someone says,

133
00:06:36,824 --> 00:06:38,750
"You made me feel worthless."

134
00:06:38,834 --> 00:06:40,250
You could respond,

135
00:06:40,334 --> 00:06:44,024
"I am sorry that my words
left you feeling worthless.

136
00:06:44,644 --> 00:06:46,400
That was not what I wanted,

137
00:06:46,484 --> 00:06:49,160
and I want to understand which part hurt."

138
00:06:49,244 --> 00:06:50,800
This acknowledges impact

139
00:06:50,884 --> 00:06:55,380
without declaring that you control
the person's entire emotional state.

140
00:06:55,464 --> 00:06:59,320
You can take responsibility for your
behavior while remembering that each

141
00:06:59,404 --> 00:07:02,960
adult is also responsible
for their interpretations,

142
00:07:03,044 --> 00:07:03,900
choices,

143
00:07:03,984 --> 00:07:05,144
and healing.

144
00:07:05,644 --> 00:07:10,124
Validation should never be
used to excuse harmful conduct.

145
00:07:10,244 --> 00:07:12,480
If someone says they are angry,

146
00:07:12,564 --> 00:07:17,250
you can validate the anger without
accepting threats or intimidation.

147
00:07:17,334 --> 00:07:19,180
"I hear that you are furious,

148
00:07:19,264 --> 00:07:21,924
and I am willing to discuss what happened.

149
00:07:22,104 --> 00:07:25,980
I will not stay in a conversation
where I am being threatened."

150
00:07:26,064 --> 00:07:27,520
If someone is jealous,

151
00:07:27,604 --> 00:07:32,484
you can understand the fear without
handing over your privacy or freedom.

152
00:07:32,724 --> 00:07:34,220
If someone is grieving,

153
00:07:34,304 --> 00:07:38,400
you can honor their pain without
promising something impossible.

154
00:07:38,484 --> 00:07:42,284
Compassion does not require
the removal of boundaries.

155
00:07:42,564 --> 00:07:47,584
There are several common responses that
sound supportive but miss the point.

156
00:07:47,754 --> 00:07:49,820
"At least it was not worse,"

157
00:07:49,904 --> 00:07:52,640
may be intended to create perspective,

158
00:07:52,724 --> 00:07:55,729
but it can make pain feel illegitimate.

159
00:07:55,813 --> 00:07:57,360
"Do not think about it,"

160
00:07:57,444 --> 00:08:02,009
asks someone to stop feeling before
they have processed what happened.

161
00:08:02,093 --> 00:08:03,840
"You are too sensitive,"

162
00:08:03,924 --> 00:08:08,349
turns a conversation about impact
into an attack on character.

163
00:08:08,433 --> 00:08:10,040
"I said I was sorry.

164
00:08:10,124 --> 00:08:11,744
What more do you want?"

165
00:08:12,164 --> 00:08:15,580
May communicate impatience
rather than repair.

166
00:08:15,664 --> 00:08:19,180
Even advice can become a form
of dismissal when it arrives

167
00:08:19,264 --> 00:08:21,564
before the person feels heard.

168
00:08:21,784 --> 00:08:25,104
Another common mistake
is forced positivity.

169
00:08:25,304 --> 00:08:26,000
Saying,

170
00:08:26,084 --> 00:08:28,300
"Everything happens for a reason,"

171
00:08:28,384 --> 00:08:31,080
or, "Just focus on the good,"

172
00:08:31,164 --> 00:08:33,980
may close down a painful conversation.

173
00:08:34,064 --> 00:08:38,380
People often need permission
to acknowledge that something was unfair,

174
00:08:38,464 --> 00:08:39,600
disappointing,

175
00:08:39,684 --> 00:08:40,620
confusing,

176
00:08:40,704 --> 00:08:41,760
or frightening.

177
00:08:41,844 --> 00:08:43,464
Hope is valuable,

178
00:08:43,564 --> 00:08:46,620
but hope should not be
used to erase reality.

179
00:08:46,704 --> 00:08:49,104
A more helpful response might be,

180
00:08:49,354 --> 00:08:50,560
"This is difficult,

181
00:08:50,644 --> 00:08:52,974
and I can see why you are struggling.

182
00:08:53,184 --> 00:08:56,600
We can think about the next
step when you are ready."

183
00:08:56,684 --> 00:09:00,340
Validation is especially
important during conflict

184
00:09:00,424 --> 00:09:04,600
because conflict activates
the urge to protect yourself.

185
00:09:04,684 --> 00:09:09,524
When accused, you may feel pressure
to prove that you are a good person.

186
00:09:09,624 --> 00:09:11,060
You may interrupt,

187
00:09:11,144 --> 00:09:13,280
list the other person's mistakes,

188
00:09:13,364 --> 00:09:16,123
or explain your intention repeatedly.

189
00:09:16,404 --> 00:09:19,554
But intention and impact
are not identical.

190
00:09:19,944 --> 00:09:22,690
Your intention tells part of the story.

191
00:09:22,774 --> 00:09:25,834
The other person's
impact tells another part.

192
00:09:26,044 --> 00:09:29,164
Mature communication has room for both.

193
00:09:29,444 --> 00:09:32,464
Consider a couple arguing about lateness.

194
00:09:32,624 --> 00:09:33,990
One partner says,

195
00:09:34,074 --> 00:09:36,364
"You do not respect my time."

196
00:09:36,744 --> 00:09:38,870
The other immediately replies,

197
00:09:38,954 --> 00:09:40,624
"That is ridiculous.

198
00:09:40,764 --> 00:09:44,994
I am late once in a while,
and you are always exaggerating."

199
00:09:45,104 --> 00:09:48,400
The conversation now
contains a character judgment,

200
00:09:48,484 --> 00:09:49,780
a counterattack,

201
00:09:49,864 --> 00:09:52,500
and a debate about the word always.

202
00:09:52,584 --> 00:09:54,790
A validating approach might be,

203
00:09:54,874 --> 00:09:56,440
"When I arrive late,

204
00:09:56,524 --> 00:10:00,144
it feels to you as if
your time does not matter to me.

205
00:10:00,404 --> 00:10:04,444
I can understand why repeated
delays would build resentment.

206
00:10:04,644 --> 00:10:08,600
I want to talk about how we can
handle plans more reliably."

207
00:10:08,684 --> 00:10:12,560
That response does not confess
to being disrespectful.

208
00:10:12,644 --> 00:10:15,280
It identifies the emotional meaning

209
00:10:15,364 --> 00:10:18,144
and moves toward a practical agreement.

210
00:10:18,924 --> 00:10:23,384
Validation can be offered through
body language as well as words.

211
00:10:23,544 --> 00:10:27,740
Face the person if that feels
safe and culturally appropriate.

212
00:10:27,824 --> 00:10:29,824
Keep your attention on them.

213
00:10:30,044 --> 00:10:33,584
Avoid a mocking smile
or an impatient sigh.

214
00:10:33,684 --> 00:10:38,424
Let your tone communicate that you are
not waiting for an opportunity to win.

215
00:10:38,724 --> 00:10:43,580
Small signals of attention can make
a difficult conversation less threatening.

216
00:10:43,664 --> 00:10:44,560
Of course,

217
00:10:44,644 --> 00:10:48,276
eye contact and touch are
not universal requirements.

218
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:50,216
Respect personal comfort,

219
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:51,016
culture,

220
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:52,356
neurodiversity,

221
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:54,880
and the context of the relationship.

222
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:57,500
You can also validate yourself.

223
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:00,896
Self-validation means recognizing your own

224
00:11:00,980 --> 00:11:05,780
feelings without treating every
feeling as a command or a fact.

225
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:07,160
You might say,

226
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:11,276
"I am anxious because this
uncertainty matters to me.

227
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,516
Anxiety does not prove that
something terrible is happening,"

228
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:20,820
or, "I am angry because I
experience that as disrespectful.

229
00:11:20,920 --> 00:11:24,056
I can decide how to respond
without attacking."

230
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:28,616
Self-validation creates a pause
between emotion and action.

231
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:33,736
It allows you to care for yourself without
demanding that another person immediately

232
00:11:33,820 --> 00:11:36,656
remove every uncomfortable feeling.

233
00:11:36,740 --> 00:11:39,276
Here is a simple checklist you can use

234
00:11:39,360 --> 00:11:42,400
when someone shares
an emotional experience.

235
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:45,160
First, have I actually listened?

236
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:46,216
Second,

237
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:50,740
can I name what they may be feeling
without mocking or minimizing it?

238
00:11:50,920 --> 00:11:51,876
Third,

239
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:56,220
have I acknowledged why that
feeling makes sense in context?

240
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:57,176
Fourth,

241
00:11:57,260 --> 00:12:00,556
have I checked whether
my understanding is correct?

242
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:01,386
Fifth,

243
00:12:01,470 --> 00:12:04,460
have I asked what kind
of support they want?

244
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:05,616
And finally,

245
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:09,860
can I share my perspective
without using it to cancel theirs?

246
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:12,676
Let us practice with a few examples.

247
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:13,956
If someone says,

248
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:16,556
"I am nervous about meeting your family,"

249
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:17,556
you could say,

250
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:19,036
"That makes sense.

251
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,916
You want to make a good impression,

252
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,440
and you do not know what to expect."

253
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:27,236
You do not have to say that
the meeting will be perfect.

254
00:12:27,320 --> 00:12:31,960
You are simply recognizing the
understandable source of the nervousness.

255
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:34,136
If a coworker says,

256
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:37,996
"I felt embarrassed when you
corrected me in front of everyone,"

257
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:39,220
you could say,

258
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:43,200
"I can see why that public
correction felt uncomfortable.

259
00:12:43,740 --> 00:12:45,556
I was focused on the mistake,

260
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:48,555
but I should have handled
the feedback privately."

261
00:12:48,639 --> 00:12:52,836
You can then discuss the work issue
without pretending the delivery had

262
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:54,320
no impact.

263
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:56,196
If a partner says,

264
00:12:56,280 --> 00:12:58,116
"I need more reassurance,"

265
00:12:58,200 --> 00:12:59,320
you could say,

266
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:02,076
"I hear that uncertainty
has been difficult,

267
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:04,616
and you want to feel secure with me.

268
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:06,056
I care about that.

269
00:13:06,140 --> 00:13:08,256
Let us talk about what reassurance would

270
00:13:08,340 --> 00:13:12,896
be helpful and what would become
unsustainable for both of us."

271
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:17,480
This recognizes the need while
leaving room for healthy limits.

272
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:19,296
If a friend says,

273
00:13:19,380 --> 00:13:22,220
"I am disappointed that you cannot come,"

274
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:23,696
you could say,

275
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:26,940
"I understand why you were
hoping I would be there.

276
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:28,976
This event matters to you,

277
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:31,556
and my absence is disappointing."

278
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:34,636
You do not need to cancel your own plans

279
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,716
simply because the friend feels let down.

280
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:42,560
Their disappointment can be real,
and your boundary can remain real.

281
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:47,476
The goal of validation is not to make
every conversation comfortable.

282
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:51,516
Sometimes being heard leads
to an uncomfortable truth.

283
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:55,956
Sometimes a person will still be
angry after you validate them.

284
00:13:56,040 --> 00:14:00,956
Sometimes validation will reveal that
the two of you want different things.

285
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:02,516
That is okay.

286
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:05,556
Validation is
not a guarantee of agreement,

287
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:06,576
forgiveness,

288
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:08,740
or a particular outcome.

289
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:10,116
It is a commitment

290
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:15,000
to treat emotional experience as
worthy of respectful attention.

291
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:20,496
There is also a limit to how much
emotional labor one person can provide.

292
00:14:20,580 --> 00:14:23,065
You cannot be available every minute.

293
00:14:23,149 --> 00:14:26,236
You cannot validate someone
while they insult you,

294
00:14:26,320 --> 00:14:27,556
manipulate you,

295
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:30,720
or demand that you deny your own reality.

296
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:31,936
You can say,

297
00:14:32,020 --> 00:14:33,936
"I want to understand you,

298
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:36,800
but I need us to speak without insults.

299
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:41,106
I am going to take a break and return
to this conversation at seven."

300
00:14:41,190 --> 00:14:45,556
A pause with a clear return
is different from abandonment.

301
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:49,696
It protects the possibility
of a better conversation.

302
00:14:49,780 --> 00:14:51,096
As you practice,

303
00:14:51,180 --> 00:14:53,656
pay attention to your own habits.

304
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:58,940
Do you rush to reassure because another
person's distress makes you uncomfortable?

305
00:14:59,140 --> 00:15:03,130
Do you debate facts because
feelings seem dangerous?

306
00:15:03,260 --> 00:15:05,696
Do you apologize automatically,

307
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:08,190
even when you need more information?

308
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:11,760
Do you use validation
to avoid setting limits?

309
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:16,820
Awareness helps you find the balance
between empathy and self-respect.

310
00:15:17,140 --> 00:15:19,696
The next time someone shares a feeling,

311
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:22,496
resist the urge to immediately correct,

312
00:15:22,580 --> 00:15:23,396
compare,

313
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:24,356
advise,

314
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:25,640
or defend.

315
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:28,220
Try three sentences instead:

316
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:30,856
I hear that this affected you.

317
00:15:30,940 --> 00:15:33,336
I can understand why it felt that way.

318
00:15:33,420 --> 00:15:37,756
I would like to understand more
before we decide what comes next.

319
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:41,796
These words do not require
you to abandon your truth.

320
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:46,360
They create enough room for two
truths to be explored carefully.

321
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:47,636
Remember,

322
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:49,956
validation is not agreement.

323
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:51,720
It is not surrender.

324
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:55,580
It is not approval
of every behavior or belief.

325
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:58,426
It is a form of relational accuracy,

326
00:15:58,510 --> 00:16:03,574
the honest recognition that another
person is having an emotional experience

327
00:16:03,658 --> 00:16:08,235
and that the experience deserves
to be understood before it is judged.

328
00:16:08,319 --> 00:16:10,055
When people feel heard,

329
00:16:10,139 --> 00:16:13,218
they are often better
able to hear you too.

330
00:16:13,899 --> 00:16:16,735
Today, share this
with someone who needs it

331
00:16:16,819 --> 00:16:21,938
and consider practicing one validating
sentence in a real conversation.

332
00:16:22,078 --> 00:16:24,899
Listen for the feeling
beneath the complaint.

333
00:16:25,138 --> 00:16:26,899
Reflect what you heard.

334
00:16:27,038 --> 00:16:29,318
Acknowledge why it makes sense.

335
00:16:29,498 --> 00:16:30,315
Then,

336
00:16:30,399 --> 00:16:32,258
when the moment is calmer,

337
00:16:32,358 --> 00:16:35,378
share your own view with honesty and care.

338
00:16:35,798 --> 00:16:40,333
That is how disagreement can
become connection instead of combat.

